Improved handling of YAML 1.1 core schema scalars, especially of floats.

Floats are now always written as valid YAML 1.1 floats, and plain scalars are
resolved according to the YAML 1.1 rules used by ASDF; see
https://yaml.org/type/float.pdf

Previously this was punted on and floats were just written with printf format
strings, but this does not guarantee correct formatting for YAML.  Floats are
also now written in the shortest form that round-trips without loss of
precision, following the same rules as Python's ``repr``.  Also fixed
locale-dependent float formatting (i.e. locale-specific decimal points).

When reading, the YAML 1.1 forms ``yes``/``no``/``on``/``off`` (bools),
``0b``/``0x``/``0``-prefixed octal, ``_`` digit separators and base-60 (ints
and floats) are now recognized.  Floats without a ``.`` such as ``1e-08`` are
still read as floats, for compatibility with files written by earlier
versions.

Strings that would otherwise be read back as another type (e.g. ``'yes'``,
``'123'``, ``'null'``) are now quoted when written, including in mapping keys.
